 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Determined by the 500,000 training steps and the cost of querying the MLP with dropout. Dropout adds negligible overhead.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard NeRF memory footprint plus storage for depth maps and uncertainty maps during training.
-*   **Strengths:** Improves geometric accuracy by leveraging depth priors; dropout prevents overfitting in the early layers.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth maps; sensitive to the choice of $\lambda$ and $\tau$; scene 710 requires a distinct hyperparameter setting.
-*   **When to use:** When depth supervision is available and geometric fidelity is prioritized over appearance alone.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of training steps ($500,000$) and the number of samples per ray [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the number of parameters in the NeRF model and the batch size [S1].
+*   **Performance:** The configuration is designed to improve geometric accuracy through depth supervision [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Explicit depth guidance improves reconstruction quality, especially in textureless regions [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires ground truth depth maps for the depth loss component [S1]. Performance may vary across scenes (e.g., scene 710 requires a different depth loss weight) [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high-quality geometry is required and ground truth depth is available [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** When depth information is unavailable or unreliable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This configuration originates from the Depth-Guided NeRF method, which utilizes Earth Mover’s Distance for depth guidance [S1]. It builds upon the original NeRF architecture [S1]. The specific hyperparameters (dropout, learning rate schedule) were tuned for datasets like ScanNet and LLFF. The DS-NeRF variant references work on sparse pose and depth refinement [S2].
+The Depth-Guided NeRF Training Configuration was introduced in the paper "Depth-Guided NeRF Training via Earth Mover’s Distance" [S1]. It builds upon the original NeRF architecture [S1]. The method was evaluated on datasets such as ScanNet and LLFF [S1, S2].
 
